  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nSeveral of the CAMs have come into better agreement with the\nconvective evolution this evening and through the overnight\nperiod. The first round, during the mid evening hours, looks to\nform off a sfc front across the eastern zones. There is a chance\nthat this activity may clip or just remain SE of Hays, Kansas.\nThe second round of storms will form off the higher terrain of \nColorado and drift across the Kansas prairie late tonight and \ninto the overnight hours. This second round may impact the \nwestern and central zones. As far as severe weather is \nconcerned, there is marginal bulk shear (30 kt) for weaker/lower\nend supercell structures... and with an eventual line out as \nthe storms develop upscale. This is for the first round. The \ninitial threats of 1-2\" hail then winds in the 60 to 70 mph \nlooks likely for the first around across the eastern zones. For \nthe second around, there could be some smaller hail, but by that\ntime in the late evening, the main threat should be more of \nconvective outflows in the 50-65 mph range. Coverage will be \nvery hit-or-miss in both rounds with high variable \nprecipitation amounts, since we are dealing with two rounds of \nthunderstorms.\n\nWinds will eventually shift out of the N to NE and blow in the\n5-20 mph range tomorrow. There will be cold air advection during\nthe day in the wake of the front with 850 hPa temperatures\ndecreasing down to around 10C by Sunday afternoon. This will\nsupport very pleasant high temperatures tomorrow with values\npeaking in the low to mid 70s across the Interstate 70 corridor\nto lower 80s across the Oklahoma border. Tomorrow would be a \ngood day to do any outdoor activities/chores given the nice \nbreak from the heat. Humidity will not be that bad either with \ndewpoints remaining in the 50s for much of the day. Monday \nmorning might be a good morning to turn off the AC, as lows \nduring this period will be in the upper 50s to around 60F. \nThere is also a low chance of showers in the morning, mainly \nconfined to the western zones.\n\nThe long range models and their ensemble counterparts are not\nshowing any major mid-level anticyclone for the first half of\nnext week. This would support the notion of more near normal\nhighs and lows Monday through Wednesday. Highs look to peak in\nthe 70s and lows look to bottom out in the 50s. There also could\nbe showers and storms developing across the higher terrain of\nColorado and then drifting across western Kansas each day. The\nhighest pops will be kept to the western zones, which is typical\nfor this type of weather pattern. More weak mid-level ridging \nmay be possible for the second half of the week with both highs \nand lows increasing.",
